1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,400
[Morning rain on a window]

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,200
<i>The lantern station, one hour before departure.</i>

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
- Mira, are you awake?
- I have been awake for hours.

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,800
That is not what I asked.

5
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:19,600
The weather chart changed again.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,800
Then we leave before the wind changes its mind.

7
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:27,700
You always make it sound simple.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,800
Simple is not the same as easy.

9
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:35,900
[A kettle whistles in the next room]

10
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:39,500
- Tea?
- Please. No sugar today.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,700
No sugar is a serious decision.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,600
We are crossing a sea, not attending a picnic.

13
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:51,900
<b>Dockmaster:</b> Final boarding call for the North Ferry.

14
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:55,600
That sounds like us.

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,700
It sounds like everyone who forgot to arrive early.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,900
- I did not forget.
- You packed three notebooks.

17
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,800
One is for maps, one is for ideas,
and one is for emergencies.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,100
What kind of emergency needs a notebook?

19
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,800
The kind where we need to remember how we got home.

20
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:19,900
[Footsteps approach along the wooden pier]

21
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:23,800
- Captain, the tide is turning.
- Then raise the ramp.

22
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:27,900
Wait! We are here.

23
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:31,700
<i>Two tickets for the far shore.</i>

24
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,900
The far shore has a name, you know.

25
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:39,800
I know. I am saving it for when we see it.

26
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:43,900
You cannot save every surprise in a notebook.

27
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:48,000
Watch me try.

28
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:52,400
[The ferry horn sounds once, low and clear]

29
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,500
- Are you afraid?
- A little. Are you?

30
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:00,600
I am afraid of standing still.

31
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:04,900
Then take my hand until the water is calm.

32
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:09,100
<u>Deal.</u>

33
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:13,200
The city lights are already smaller.

34
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:17,500
That only means the horizon has more room.

35
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:21,600
- You really believe that?
- I need to today.

36
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,700
[Rain fades beneath the sound of the engine]

37
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,100
We should write down the first thing we see.

38
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:34,200
Only if we also write down what it feels like.

39
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:38,700
That will take more than one notebook.

40
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Good. We brought three.

41
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:46,100
I left the lantern burning

42
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,100
because I thought

43
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:52,000
we would find our way back before dark.

44
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,500
[A radio crackles on the bridge]

45
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,500
I know I sound excited, but...

46
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:01,100
...I am worried about the crossing.

47
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Our neighbors are expecting their second child.

48
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,200
Tomorrow I will step onto the deck

49
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:10,100
with a confident smile

50
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:13,300
and find a way to help the crew.

51
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Yes, I am all in on your plan.
